  <h2>How this was built</h2>

  <p>
    The pipeline is Python. Source files are read with a streaming parser so multi-gigabyte
    tables do not have to fit in memory, and every acquisition sits behind a source
    interface so the way data arrives can change without touching anything downstream.
  </p>

  <p>
    The site is a static Astro build that ships no JavaScript on pages that do not need
    it. Every figure it displays is read at build time from a record the pipeline
    generated, which means a number on a page cannot drift from the record behind it —
    if the pipeline reruns and a count changes, the page changes with it. There is no
    step where a human retypes a figure into a template.
  </p>

  <h2>A note on what went wrong</h2>

  <p>
    The original brief named PatentsView as the primary data source. By the time
    building started, the PatentsView API had been retired and its data moved to the
    USPTO Open Data Portal. Separately, the first draft of the classification mapping
    used CPC codes that had been retired in a scheme reorganisation and would have
    silently matched nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both were caught by checking rather than assuming, and both are documented in the
    repository rather than quietly corrected. That is the habit this portfolio is
    meant to demonstrate: the failure mode worth defending against is not the error
    that breaks the build, it is the error that produces a plausible number.
  </p>

  <h2>Reproducing it</h2>

  <p>
    The repository is public and the README explains how to reproduce every result,
    including which files to download, what to record about them, and which command
    regenerates each artifact.
  </p>
</Base>
